## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design real-time data quality and drift detection at scale, balancing schema evolution, validation, quarantine, alerting, and cross-account governance using AWS services.

## Key Concepts
- Schema evolution and registry management
- Real-time validation and data quarantining
- Observability: CloudWatch metrics and SNS alerts
- Cross-account auditability via Glue Data Catalog and Lake Formation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo validator for a streaming record
function isRecordValid(r){
  if(!r.user_id || !r.timestamp) return false;
  if(typeof r.value !== 'number' || r.value < 0) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift detection across regions and accounts?
- What are trade-offs of using a separate quarantine bucket vs inline rejection for analytics workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical, production-ready thinking: streaming ingestion, schema evolution, idempotency, late-arriving data, and end-to-end testing under budget constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming ingestion with Kinesis Data Streams and optional Firehose path
- Glue Schema Registry for versioned JSON schemas and evolution
- Parquet with daily partitions in S3
- Idempotent writes via per-event dedup keys stored (e.g., DynamoDB)
- Late data handling with watermarking and bounded backfill
- End-to-end testing with synthetic bursts, replay, and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lambda-like pseudo-code for dedupe check
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const id = event.id;
  const exists = await ddb.get({TableName:'dedupe', Key:{id}}).promise();
  if (exists.Item) return {status:'duplicate'};
  await ddb.put({TableName:'dedupe', Item:{id}}).promise();
  // proceed with validation and write to Parquet path in S3
  return {status:'ok'};
};
```

